WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
The first film followed inherited evidence to a surprising pair.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.880
Once evidence is scored, how does a branching tree turn it into a claim about

3
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.460
relatives? The answer is not the nearest tip.

4
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.840
It is the path back to a shared branch point.

5
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:21.843
Begin with a trait table.

6
00:00:23.163 --> 00:00:27.243
Each row is an organism, and each column is one inherited feature.

7
00:00:28.503 --> 00:00:30.203
A present mark lights up in teal.

8
00:00:31.443 --> 00:00:35.563
When the same marks form a smaller group inside a larger group, the groups are

9
00:00:35.563 --> 00:00:38.603
nested. A tree can show that nesting.

10
00:00:39.683 --> 00:00:43.023
A useful marker is a shared derived characteristic.

11
00:00:44.403 --> 00:00:49.003
It first appeared in one ancestor and passed to that ancestor's descendants.

12
00:00:50.303 --> 00:00:53.103
Move its mark from the table onto the matching branch.

13
00:00:54.363 --> 00:00:58.063
Every tip beyond that mark belongs to the marked group in this tree.

14
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:04.373
A branch point, also called a node, represents a common ancestral population.

15
00:01:05.653 --> 00:01:08.533
Start at two tips and trace both lines toward the root.

16
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:12.213
Stop at the first node shared by both paths.

17
00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:17.393
A pair sharing a later node is more closely related in this tree.

18
00:01:18.853 --> 00:01:22.813
Trace shared derived traits to branch points to find the closest relatives.

19
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:27.527
Now spin the whale and hippo branches around their shared node.

20
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:29.927
The tips trade places.

21
00:01:31.187 --> 00:01:34.347
Did the relationship change, or did only the drawing move?

22
00:01:35.627 --> 00:01:37.507
Follow both paths before you decide.

23
00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:44.507
Only the drawing moved.

24
00:01:45.887 --> 00:01:49.867
Both paths still meet at the same node, so the relationship is unchanged.

25
00:01:51.447 --> 00:01:53.447
Tip order cannot decide relatedness.

26
00:01:54.587 --> 00:01:56.667
This tree also has no top or goal.

27
00:01:58.027 --> 00:02:02.007
Every living tip has continued changing since its ancestors split.

28
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.760
Branch length needs a key.

29
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.700
On a plain cladogram, a long line can be empty space for layout.

30
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.120
It does not automatically mean more time or more change.

31
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.720
Some trees add a scale for time or change.

32
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.040
Read that scale before comparing lengths.

33
00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:25.973
A tree can only use the organisms and evidence included.

34
00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:30.693
New fossils, sequences, or traits can test its branches.

35
00:02:32.153 --> 00:02:37.393
Different evidence may disagree, so scientists compare several lines and treat the

36
00:02:37.393 --> 00:02:38.853
tree as a supported model.

37
00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:45.013
Scientists can update that model when stronger evidence changes the supported

38
00:02:45.013 --> 00:02:50.773
branching order. The clean classroom table is a starting case, not every case.

39
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:53.647
So do not read across the tips.

40
00:02:55.207 --> 00:03:00.567
Trace backward. Find the first node shared by both paths, then ask which shared

41
00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.367
derived trait marks that branch.

42
00:03:03.807 --> 00:03:07.767
Rotation changes the picture, and a scale can change what length means.

43
00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:11.267
The branching order carries the relationship.
